tackle is: Why is my ice cream too hard?&nbsp;

00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:06.480
When ice cream goes too hard in the freezer,&nbsp;
too hard to scoop, too hard to spoon and eat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:12.440
that is generally down to one of two things.&nbsp;
Number one is there's not enough sugar in there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:17.920
and number two is there's way too much fat. When&nbsp;
you make ice cream, there is a specific amount&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:23.800
of sugar required to get the texture that you're&nbsp;
after. It's the same if you use a sugar substitute&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:33.080
as well so it it's a balance of getting the right&nbsp;
amount of freezing depression vs your recipe and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:39.080
this is where terms like POD and PAC come in. POD&nbsp;
is the amount of sweetness, PAC is how hard it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.080 --> 00:01:46.800
freezes, so if you use a perfectly balanced recipe&nbsp;
and you've balanced it so that it is scoopable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:52.200
straight from your freezer it means the the amount&nbsp;
of sugar and or sugar substitute is perfect for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:56.240
your recipe and the amount of solids that you&nbsp;
have in there. If you start playing with that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.240 --> 00:01:59.960
let's say you use sugar and you think actually&nbsp;
I don't want to use that much sugar because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:04.680
to me that's too much, you start taking that&nbsp;
Sugar out, let's say you take half your sugar out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:11.000
of your ice cream recipe you've removed half the&nbsp;
solids and you've subtracted half of the freezing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:15.360
point depression ingredient from your ice cream&nbsp;
which means it's going to go much much harder in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:23.200
your freezer. So you need to understand what the&nbsp;
ingredients do and their effect on your ice cream.&nbsp;

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:29.520
Question number two is: My ice cream&nbsp;
is too soft or it won't freeze at all?&nbsp;

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:35.000
Again this actually is exactly the polar opposite&nbsp;
to the last question, this is you've got too much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:40.840
sugar and or you've affected the freezing point&nbsp;
too much. So if you use something like Xylitol&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:46.200
which has a strong effect on the freezing point or&nbsp;
Erythritol, which is awful in ice cream, that has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:51.360
a really strong effect on a freezing point. If you&nbsp;
use too much of these and you cook your recipe and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:57.440
you put it in a freezer for a ninja creamy and&nbsp;
it doesn't freeze and or you put it in your ice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.440 --> 00:03:04.400
cream machine and it doesn't churn, it's because&nbsp;
you've brought the freezing point down so low that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:10.280
your freezer can't freeze it, that your ice cream&nbsp;
machine can't freeze it. The walls of an ice cream&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.280 --> 00:03:15.080
machine like a standard home churning machine&nbsp;
get down to about minus 20°C - 25°C depending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.080 --> 00:03:22.600
on the machine so you have to really go some to&nbsp;
stop it freezing. Your standard freezer at home&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.600 --> 00:03:29.160
is somewhere around -17°C minus 18°C again that's&nbsp;
actually surprisingly easy to get it below that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:35.600
point, you think "okay I'm going to use a sugar&nbsp;
substitute" this recipe calls for 150g of table&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:42.080
sugar I'll add 150g of Erthritol, it it won't&nbsp;
freeze. It will have a freezing point of about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:49.720
-30°C so it won't freeze at all and you you know&nbsp;
when when people come on and say "oh my my ice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.720 --> 00:03:53.440
cream didn't freeze your recipe is rubbish"&nbsp;
what did you.... my first question is what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:58.960
did you change, because they change something&nbsp;
and here we come with the "oh I didn't want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:05.160
use sugar I want to use Erthritol" you can't make&nbsp;
these drastic changes to recipes, it's the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.160 --> 00:04:08.560
for baking as well, you can't just substitute&nbsp;
something for something else and expect it to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:15.960
be the same. You need to understand what they do.
Question number three is: My ice cream is too icy?&nbsp;

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:22.640
Ice crystals form for a couple of reasons,&nbsp;
ice cream itself is a balance of ice crystals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:28.440
air bubbles and fat that that's what creates ice&nbsp;
cream with the right size ice crystals the right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:32.920
amount of air Incorporated and the right amount&nbsp;
of fat you get this perfect texture. When your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.920 --> 00:04:40.320
ice cream is too icy it means your ice crystals&nbsp;
have been allowed to increase in size more than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:46.760
we would like. That happens for a few reasons,&nbsp;
one is there's not enough sugar to to counter that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.760 --> 00:04:52.120
freezing process number two is the ingredients&nbsp;
like to recrystallize after you've made the ice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.120 --> 00:04:57.440
cream and number three is you haven't stabilized&nbsp;
any of that free water so if you you if you make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.440 --> 00:05:03.320
an ice cream and it has 60% water content, that&nbsp;
water needs to be stabilized in a couple of ways&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.320 --> 00:05:09.240
one is either you can create a sugar syrup and&nbsp;
stabilize the water with the sugar and that will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.240 --> 00:05:14.680
help reduce the freezing point of your water and&nbsp;
slow the Ice Crystal formation which will then in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.680 --> 00:05:20.480
turn make the Ice Crystal smaller or you stabilize&nbsp;
it with something like Guar gum, Xanthan gum,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:27.760
Tara gum any of those you know, Locust bean gum,&nbsp;
caraggenan all these all these stabilizers what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:34.360
they do is they bond to the water and create&nbsp;
a more viscous liquid which then reduces the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.360 --> 00:05:39.640
Ice Crystal formation. Another reason ice crystals&nbsp;
form is the amount of time it takes to churn your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.640 --> 00:05:44.240
ice cream, in a traditional machine if you have&nbsp;
a like a freezer bowl one where you put it in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.240 --> 00:05:49.080
the freezer for 24 hours and bring it out, they&nbsp;
are historically much slower at churning than a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.080 --> 00:05:57.000
high-end compressor machine. The slower the churn&nbsp;
the larger the ice crystals get so if you were to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.000 --> 00:06:03.920
take my musso, which is one I use on the videos&nbsp;
here, that will churn in about 12 to 15 minutes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.920 --> 00:06:08.640
depending on the recipe if you take a freezer bowl&nbsp;
version it might take 20 to 25 minutes your ice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.640 --> 00:06:12.880
crystals are going to be larger than they are&nbsp;
in mine. There's nothing we can do about that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.880 --> 00:06:19.240
by balancing the recipe to suit your equipment&nbsp;
is the best way to combat that, so if you know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.240 --> 00:06:24.640
that your ice cream machine churns slow you can&nbsp;
reduce the sugar ever so slightly and that makes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.640 --> 00:06:30.640
it freeze quicker because it has a slightly higher&nbsp;
freezing point I also recommend it looking into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.640 --> 00:06:35.760
using stabilizers Gura gum, Xanthan gum you&nbsp;
know Tara gum, they're excellent even if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.760 --> 00:06:39.640
don't look at any of the others and you don't look&nbsp;
at combining them those three are really really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.640 --> 00:06:45.280
good for making your ice cream much much better.&nbsp;
The next question is: My ice cream is not sweet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.280 --> 00:06:52.280
enough? this generally comes from people that do&nbsp;
"no sugar zero sugar low sugar" recipes the sugar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.280 --> 00:07:00.720
substitutes generally are not as sweet as sugar&nbsp;
and that is the POD, POD that is what that means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:09.480
it's sweetness equivalency essentially compared to&nbsp;
sucrose so Allulose is like 70% as sweet as table&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.480 --> 00:07:16.360
sugar xylitol is about 65 to 70% as sweet as table&nbsp;
sugar all these kind of things when you start&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.360 --> 00:07:21.080
using them in recipes and your ice cream isn't&nbsp;
sweet enough to taste you can boost the sweetness&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.080 --> 00:07:26.720
using something like Stevia where you really only&nbsp;
