00:07:26.720 --> 00:07:32.280
the size of your recipe to increase the sweetness&nbsp;
or apparent sweetness, perceived sweetness to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.280 --> 00:07:37.560
level that's in line with traditional table sugar.
Next question is: My ice cream gets icy after a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.560 --> 00:07:43.760
couple of days? this happens again for a few&nbsp;
reasons mainly one of two. One is that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.760 --> 00:07:47.560
continually open your freezer, open and close,&nbsp;
open and close, what you're doing is you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.560 --> 00:07:52.920
thawing your ice cream ever so slightly when you&nbsp;
open it because you're removing all that cold air&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.920 --> 00:07:59.520
and then you are closing it and it's refreezing&nbsp;
again, you create that freeze thaw cycle and every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.520 --> 00:08:04.720
every time you freeze and thaw, freeze and thaw,&nbsp;
the ice crystals will increase in size. If you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.720 --> 00:08:12.760
have a frost-free freezer then that most of the&nbsp;
time, every night those things go up to a certain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.760 --> 00:08:18.320
temperature to melt the ice and then they freeze&nbsp;
back down that will have a huge impact on your ice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.320 --> 00:08:24.720
cream. I hate frost free fridge freezers for that&nbsp;
reason they destroy the texture of your ice cream&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.720 --> 00:08:29.880
and sorbet. And generally the other reason is&nbsp;
stabilization there's too much free water in there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.880 --> 00:08:34.800
and as an Ice Crystal starts to form over time it&nbsp;
will just get bigger and bigger and bigger because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.800 --> 00:08:39.720
there's too much unprocessed water in a recipe&nbsp;
and that's why we need to look at stabilizing that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.720 --> 00:08:46.120
water, thicken it so that it doesn't get larger&nbsp;
and larger in size and stays luscious, silky&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.120 --> 00:08:53.160
and smooth like the ice cream that we all enjoy.
The next question is: Crumbly texture to my ice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.160 --> 00:09:00.840
cream? this is usually caused by too high solids,&nbsp;
it can also be caused by too high milk solids you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.840 --> 00:09:05.200
can get a crumbly texture in two different ways&nbsp;
one when you go and scoop it it doesn't scoop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.200 --> 00:09:11.160
a nice smooth creamy ball, it breaks apart&nbsp;
